WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.491
Different tasks require an object
to be held in different ways.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.168
Humans often change their grasp
while moving the object into place.

00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:15.520
In this paper, we present DexGO: dexterous
grasp transition with object relocation.

00:00:16.183 --> 00:00:19.113
Our policy is given only the final goal,

00:00:19.163 --> 00:00:21.113
involving a target grasp

00:00:21.163 --> 00:00:23.113
and a desired object pose.

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:27.920
The motion in between has to
be discovered by the policy.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.445
Such complex motions are
hard to learn from scratch.

00:00:31.741 --> 00:00:33.161
To deal with this challenge,

00:00:33.161 --> 00:00:37.421
we identify four basic capabilities
that such motions require:

00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:39.313
in-hand reorientation,

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.380
in-hand translation,

00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:43.477
grasp type change,

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:45.735
and object relocation.

00:00:46.714 --> 00:00:51.234
A complex motion can then be modeled as
a combination of these capabilities.

00:00:51.534 --> 00:00:54.871
Each capability and each
combination is a task,

00:00:54.871 --> 00:00:57.474
and together they form
a prerequisite graph.

00:00:57.874 --> 00:01:01.864
Initially, only the four
single-capability tasks are trained.

00:01:02.164 --> 00:01:06.824
A combination task is unlocked once all
its prerequisites have been learned.

00:01:09.463 --> 00:01:12.558
In this way, simpler tasks
provide intermediate guidance

00:01:12.558 --> 00:01:14.813
for learning the more complex ones.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:22.537
In the end, a single policy
has learned all fifteen tasks.

00:01:23.617 --> 00:01:27.437
Goals also vary widely in
difficulty and distance:

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:29.517
from a small in-hand adjustment,

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:32.687
to a grasp type change
with distant relocation.

00:01:32.887 --> 00:01:37.234
So one policy has to make both large
movements and fine adjustments.

00:01:37.983 --> 00:01:41.523
The reward has to guide the
policy across this whole range.

00:01:41.723 --> 00:01:44.573
We therefore propose an
annealed kernel reward.

00:01:44.673 --> 00:01:49.053
At first, the kernel is wide,
to guide the policy from far away.

00:01:49.153 --> 00:01:51.023
As the policy improves,

00:01:51.073 --> 00:01:52.845
it is narrowed for precision.

00:01:53.283 --> 00:01:55.803
Some tasks are learned sooner than others.

00:01:56.003 --> 00:01:59.253
So each task narrows its
kernel at its own pace,

00:01:59.403 --> 00:02:02.641
and counts as learned once
its kernel is narrow enough.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.476
Trained with DexGO, our policy completes
grasp type changes where prior work fails.

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:17.763
The policy generalizes to
unseen everyday objects,

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.269
including tools and household items.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:32.120
The framework can also be applied
to a different hand morphology,

00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:36.819
with the same training settings.

00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:50.225
The policy can even complete goals on
objects far outside the training size range.

00:02:53.683 --> 00:02:56.826
Different target grasps
lead to distinct motions,

00:02:56.826 --> 00:03:00.815
even under the same initial
state and desired object pose.

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:07.851
Finally, by chaining goals, the policy
performs long-horizon manipulation,

00:03:07.851 --> 00:03:12.521
reaching one goal after another on the
same object, each from a different task.

00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:16.300
In summary, DexGO builds
a prerequisite graph

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.614
and introduces a task-wise
annealed kernel reward,

00:03:19.614 --> 00:03:24.923
achieving dexterous grasp transition with
object relocation across diverse goals.
